    "title": "Why is Friday the 13th unlucky? See origin, 7 superstitions to know",
    "text": "RICKMANSWORTH, ENGLAND - SEPTEMBER 08: Jason Voorhees' (C.J Graham) Hero Hockey Mask from Friday The 13th Part VI: Jason Lives, est Â£40,000-60,000 goes on view at the Propstore on September 08, 2022 in Rickmansworth, England. Over 1,500 rare and iconic lots will be sold during Propstoreâs Entertainment Memorabilia Live Auction over four days from Thursday 3rd to Sunday 6th November 2022. (Photo by Tristan Fewings/Getty Images)Tristan Fewings/Getty ImagesIt’s the first Friday the 13th of 2026 — the first of three this year — which either means you’re stoked to get a Friday the 13th tattoo or you’re cautiously avoiding bad vibes. Or maybe both. Or maybe you just want to celebrate Galentine's Day and ignore anything spooky or superstitious.Watch MoreWhat's the history of Friday the 13th? Why is it unlucky?Friday the 13th’s reputation for bad luck largely originates from religious and cultural beliefs.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢According to History.com, 13 guests attended the Last Supper — where Jesus Christ and his 12 apostles dined the day before his crucifixion on Good Friday. The 13th guest, Judas, betrayed Jesus. Historians say this helped spark a Christian superstition that it’s bad luck to dine with 13 people at a table. Combined with the belief that Jesus died on a Friday, the date took on an ominous meaning in Christian tradition.The number 13 has long been associated with negative omens. In Norse mythology, for example, Loki is said to have arrived as the 13th guest at a feast in Valhalla, bringing chaos and death. Meanwhile, 12 is often seen as a number of completeness — 12 months in a year, 12 zodiac signs, 12 days of Christmas — making 13 feel like an unsettling disruption.The superstition deepened over time, particularly after the mass arrest of the Knights Templar on Friday, Oct. 13, 1307, which some historians say further cemented the day’s unlucky reputation.Friday the 13th on a calendar. Image from KDAF.Austin American-StatesmanWhen is the next Friday the 13th?We’ve got two Friday the 13ths back-to-back this year — February and March — with a third in November. It’s the maximum possible in a single calendar year.Article continues below this adCurious black cat with bright green eyes looking directly at the camera in a green garden settingOliver Helbig/Getty Images7 Friday the 13th superstitions to either avoid or pursueTo avoid:Statesman LogoMake us a Preferred Source on Google to see more of us when you search.Add Preferred Source Breaking a mirror: It's said that breaking a mirror will bring seven years of bad luck. Many ancient cultures believed reflections held pieces of a person’s soul. Walking under a ladder: This superstition comes from the idea that a ladder forms a triangle — a sacred symbol in some traditions — and walking through it invites misfortune. Spilling salt: Once a rare and valuable commodity, salt was associated with purity. To counteract bad luck, toss a pinch over your left shoulder. Crossing paths with a black cat: In folklore, black cats were often linked to witches and bad omens. Stepping on cracks: Folklore suggests cracks in the ground are portals to the supernatural. The rhyme “step on a crack, break your mother’s back” helped cement the idea. Article continues below this adTo pursue: Carrying a lucky charm: Four-leaf clovers, horseshoes and rabbit’s feet are classic symbols of good fortune. For some, a lucky charm can be anything sentimental — a rock, crystal or religious item. Knocking on wood: This tradition is believed to ward off bad luck or prevent a jinx, rooted in ancient beliefs that spirits lived in trees. — Former Austin American-Statesman reporter Brandi Addison contributed to this report.Article continues below this adStatesman LogoGet alerted to the most important stories and stay on top of the news.Feb 13, 2026Breaking & Trending EditorKatey Psencik is the breaking and trending editor at the Austin American-Statesman. A Central Texas native and proud graduate of the University of Texas at Austin, Katey's journalism career spans print, TV, digital media, and nonprofits. When she's not on the news desk, you can find her teaching journalism classes at UT, reading romance novels, hunting for the best dive bar in South Austin or snuggling with her two dogs, Oso and Mav.",
